meta {
  name: Reconcile
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

post {
  url: {{groupAdmin}}/reconcile
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "path": "{{groupPrefix}}{{streamName}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("runs the leases of the group that path belongs to", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(204);
  });
}

docs {
  # Reconcile

  Runs a group's leases against the relay again, named by a path rather than by a group key.

  This is what the relay's own read hook calls, which is why it takes no key: a starting read announces the path it is on, and the run goes against the leases the members themselves stated.
  It grants nothing a key would have bought, and a group with no live member is left alone.

  The run answers no content.
  This is the one route asking for no credential, and a path is public, so an answer describing the group would be one anybody holding a path could read: the members' names, how many are live, and which connections went.

  What a run did is read off the scrape instead, which the deployment gates: `groupd_kicks_total`, `groupd_kicks_failed_total` and `groupd_relay_lists_unread_total` (`groupd -metrics`).
  The service logs a run that closed anything.

  Doing it by hand is how a run is triggered without waiting for a reader to arrive.
  The hook is `deploy/reconcile-on-read.sh`, wired in `pathDefaults.runOnRead`.

  A path belonging to no group is refused with 400, a bare stream name naming no group.
}
